<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Fort-Dauphin Colony</h1><p class="st-meta">1643 CE – 1674 CE · capital Fort-Dauphin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's first colony on Madagascar, a struggling trading post on the south-east coast that ended in massacre and evacuation in 1674.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1643 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-de-lorient">French Company of the East</a> (Compagnie de l'Orient) sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-pronis">Jacques Pronis</a> to establish a post in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taolagnaro">Fort-Dauphin</a>, named after the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, in the lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/antanosy-kingdom">Antanosy Kingdom</a> near modern Taolagnaro.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony was small, poorly supplied and frequently mutinous, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> obtained from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanosy">Antanosy</a> by trade or raiding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etienne-de-flacourt">Etienne de Flacourt</a>, governor from 1648 to 1655, wrote a detailed history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. Colonists also settled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Bourbon</a> (Reunion), and the post came under the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> in 1664.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Mounting conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanosy">Antanosy</a> culminated in an attack in 1674, after which the surviving French evacuated to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ile-bourbon">Bourbon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> later used the episode to justify its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1643 CE – 1674 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.40%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1643 CE – 1674 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort-Dauphin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jacques Pronis, Etienne de Flacourt</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/madagascar">Madagascar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fort-Dauphin, Tolagnaro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=fort-dauphin-colony" aria-label="Fort-Dauphin Colony on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5955 2862 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Fort-Dauphin Colony lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6275 3118h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6275 3118h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Madagascar. <a href="/atlas?year=1658&amp;focus=fort-dauphin-colony">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

